%N A399325 Decimal expansion of the root in (0,1) of 7^(x-1) = 2^x - 1.
%C A399325 x = 1 is always a solution of 7^(x-1) = 2^x - 1; this constant c is the unique other real solution in (0,1).
%C A399325 Conjecturally the number of odd terms of A267703 that are <= X grows like X^(c+o(1)). The defining equation is the first-moment condition of a branching-process model of the tree of preimages of 1 under the 7x+1 map: an odd m has odd preimages (2^v*m - 1)/7 only when m == 1, 2 or 4 (mod 7), and then v runs through one residue class mod 3. For the 5x+1 map the same equation, 5^(x-1) = 2^x - 1, gives 0.650919..., the exponent of the branching-process model of Kontorovich and Lagarias (Theorem 8.10).
%C A399325 In the maximum formula, setting the derivative to zero gives (1-b)/b = 7^(1-c), and substituting back gives the defining equation; the maximizer is b = 1 - 2^(-c) = 0.2280929895....
%C A399325 The numbers of odd n <= 10^4, 10^8, 10^12, 10^16 whose 7x+1 trajectory reaches 1 are 14, 258, 6337, 201248 (lower bounds, unchanged for trajectory ceilings from 10^10*X up to 10^32). The local slopes over the windows [10^12, 10^16] and [10^16, 10^20] are 0.3755 and 0.3729.
%H A399325 Karan Sharma, <a href="/A399325/b399325.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..999</a>
%H A399325 A. V. Kontorovich and J. C. Lagarias, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/0910.1944">Stochastic models for the 3x+1 and 5x+1 problems and related problems</a>, arXiv:0910.1944 [math.NT], 2009; in: The Ultimate Challenge: The 3x+1 Problem, Amer. Math. Soc., 2010, pp. 131-188.
%F A399325 c is the root in (0,1) of 7^(c-1) = 2^c - 1, i.e., of 1 + 7^(c-1) = 2^c.
%F A399325 c = max_{0 < b < 1/log_2(7)} (H(b) - b*log_2(7))/(1 - b*log_2(7)), where H(b) = -b*log_2(b) - (1-b)*log_2(1-b); the maximum is attained only at b = 1 - 2^(-c).
%F A399325 1/(7*log(2)) < c < 2/(7*log(2)).
%e A399325 0.3735010344307703694723501267483508012036581072424486...
%o A399325 (Python)
%o A399325 from mpmath import mp, mpf, findroot
%o A399325 mp.dps = 110
%o A399325 c = findroot(lambda x: mpf(7)**(x-1) - mpf(2)**x + 1, mpf('0.37'))
%o A399325 print([int(d) for d in mp.nstr(c, 106).split('.')[1][:105]])
%Y A399325 Cf. A020860 (log_2(7)), A267703 (numbers whose 7x+1 trajectory reaches 1), A396960 (15x+1 analog).
